<div class="bb-quote"><b>Foote wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">(edit)....That question is a little less clear when we are talking about using Overdrive out of turn. Though I would also guess that the intent is that if you use Overdrive out of turn, it more or less becomes an instant use of both Strikes. That to me is the least convoluted way to look at it.
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
This seems to be the cleanest way to do it.&nbsp;

[/quote]

I would have to agree. It seems to me that the double Strike would happen immediately as a result of resolving the effects of the card. This gets a little confused by the use of &quot;this turn&quot; at the end which can be interpreted to imply it can be used at any point in the turn, but I think that &quot;this turn&quot; is added there to limit the number of times you can use it and not to defer the use of Strike to a later time within the turn.&nbsp;

Again, that being said, I cannot offer a definitive argument against playing Overdrive, doing everything else in the turn and then using Strike twice... so if that is allowed or disallowed, it should probably be clarified.&nbsp;
